00:00:02.556 --> 00:00:06.670
The way memory works is
in terms of snapshots.

00:00:06.670 --> 00:00:12.700
I don't remember the entire experience,
I remember moments in that experience.

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:13.970
And why is that so critical?

00:00:13.970 --> 00:00:18.300
Because it tells you that what you
need to manage is not the film, but

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:21.790
it's a specific kind of moment,
where people most remember is that end.

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:27.630
Overall, people rate colonoscopies
that take longer, but end less

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:32.475
painfully better than colonoscopies
that are shorter, but end more

00:00:32.475 --> 00:00:37.010
painfully.33 It doesn't matter how long
something took, it matters how it ended.

00:00:37.010 --> 00:00:40.140
They remember the end, which we've talked
about, but they also remember the peak.

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:41.890
It's something called the Peak and
End Rule.

00:00:41.890 --> 00:00:43.980
People take those two
pieces of information and

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:45.780
that's how they remember an experience.

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:49.930
You can think of, you go to Disney,
you go on the ride, that's an up, and

00:00:49.930 --> 00:00:51.620
then you wait in line, that's a down.

00:00:51.620 --> 00:00:54.350
You go from sort of location to location.

00:00:54.350 --> 00:00:58.500
It's not kind of a uniform experience,
it has all kinds of spikes, okay?

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:03.560
And it has kind of moments in which
experiences naturally break up.

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:05.840
So if you think in terms of peak and end,

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:10.960
you want to break up an experience when
you're near the top of something, right?

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:14.560
So then, when I think back as
a customer about my entire experience,

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:18.430
what I've coded in my head
is a series of peak moments.

00:01:18.430 --> 00:01:22.420
A lot of companies forget
the importance of finishing strong.

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:25.870
Hotels, they'll do a lot for
you when you come in, but

00:01:25.870 --> 00:01:28.510
then, when you check out,
that process isn't really managed.

00:01:28.510 --> 00:01:29.971
Right?
It would actually be smart and

00:01:29.971 --> 00:01:32.960
appropriate for
them to give you a departure gift.

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:36.950
So remember, any kind of customer
experience that you have,

00:01:36.950 --> 00:01:41.459
go back to your desk after this webinar
and ask yourself, how do we finish?

00:01:41.459 --> 00:01:42.740
How does this thing end?

